A warning changes the later act before the act occurs. Before the warning, an agent lacks a particular piece of knowledge. After it, the same action can be described as action taken \emph{despite knowing}. This is why Loney's account of \term{atasthalia} matters: the word repeatedly carries moral censure, and foreknowledge of destructive consequences is central to that censure \citep{Loney2015}. The warning is not the efficient cause of the later slaughter. It changes the epistemic and normative conditions under which the slaughter will be judged.

This helps explain why warnings survive their own failure. In Book 2 Halitherses interprets Zeus's eagles as a sign that Odysseus is near and destruction is coming for the suitors (Od. 2.145--76). They reject him. In Book 24, after the suitors are dead, Halitherses can remind the Ithacans that he and Mentor tried to restrain their sons before disaster arrived (Od. 24.451--66). The speech that failed to change behavior at the beginning has become evidence at the end. It now supports the claim: they were told.

The poem even gives us a partial control case. Halitherses warns the assembly again in Book 24, this time against attacking Odysseus. More than half the men leave instead of following Eupeithes into another cycle of retaliation (Od. 24.466--71). The seer who was ignored before the consequence is obeyed after the consequence has validated his authority. Warnings are not structurally doomed to fail. Their efficacy depends on credibility, circumstances, and what the audience has already suffered.

That distinction matters for Nolan. Athena's rebuke, ``Tiresias told you. Circe told you. The gods told you,'' initially sounds as though warning settles responsibility. But warning answers only one question: what was known? It does not tell us what alternatives remained when action occurred.

\section{The cattle: they chose, but between what?}

The crew's provisions run out. The wind holds them on the island. Hunger becomes severe. Eurylochus persuades the men to kill the cattle by explicitly comparing outcomes: starving slowly is a dreadful death; if the gods destroy them at sea, that death is preferable (Od. 12.339--52).

The scene is more difficult than a simple opposition between obedience and disobedience. The men know the prohibition. They deliberately violate it. But the cost of obedience has changed. At the beginning of the stop, ``do not eat the cattle'' means: eat your own stores instead. Later it means: continue to abstain while starving.

This is why the bare statement \emph{they chose} is insufficient. They did choose. The harder question is what their choice set had become.

Aristotle, writing centuries later, gives useful comparative language for this problem. In \term{Nicomachean Ethics} III.1 he calls some actions under severe external pressure ``mixed'': a sailor does not want, simply speaking, to throw cargo overboard, but chooses to do so in a storm to save lives. The action is chosen in the circumstances without being desirable in itself (EN 1110a4--19). Aristotle cannot be used as a dictionary for Homer, but the comparison clarifies what the cattle episode already dramatizes: voluntariness and constraint can coexist.

The Homeric episode therefore separates at least three questions. Did the crew physically perform the act? Yes. Did they know it was forbidden and dangerous? Yes. Did the surrounding conditions affect the significance of the alternatives? Also yes. Responsibility cannot be read straight off the fact that a decision occurred.

Nolan preserves the complication by letting Eurylochus interrupt Athena's philosophy with food. The interruption is not a distraction from the question of consequence. It is the question becoming concrete.

\section{Leadership is not the same as control}

Odysseus remembers himself as the man who led the crew to its end. Homer gives us a more granular picture of that leadership.

At Thrinakia Odysseus wants to sail past the island. Eurylochus objects. The crew agrees with Eurylochus. Odysseus acknowledges that the many are forcing him while he stands alone (Od. 12.271--98). Unable to enforce the first plan, he changes the architecture of the situation: if they must land, they must swear an oath not to harm the cattle. Leadership becomes negotiation because direct command has failed.

Scylla is different. There Odysseus possesses information the crew does not. Circe has told him that six men will die. He deliberately withholds that warning because he fears the men would stop rowing in terror (Od. 12.223--59). At Scylla he controls information. At Thrinakia he does not control the collective decision to land. Both episodes involve leadership, but not the same kind or degree of control.

This is why Odysseus's later statement ``I led them'' is too coarse to settle responsibility. The category \emph{leader} does not tell us who knew what, who could veto what, who selected the route, who controlled information, or who retained practical alternatives.

Bernard Williams's account of Homeric responsibility helps resist the false choice between modern free will and archaic fatalism. Williams argues that Homer already has robust ``centres of agency'' and recognizable practices of responsibility even when divine and human explanations overlap \citep[chs.~2--3]{Williams2008}. A god's involvement does not automatically erase the human act; a human act does not require the human to be the ultimate cause of the world in which it occurs.

That is close to what Nolan's Athena forces Odysseus to confront. He does not need to be the first cause to be answerable. He also does not become the first cause by accepting answerability.

\section{The Cyclops: consequence needs an address}

The Cyclops episode gives a cleaner causal chain. Odysseus and his men have escaped. His companions beg him not to keep taunting Polyphemus. Odysseus ignores them and reveals his real name. Only then can Polyphemus address a specific prayer to Poseidon: if Odysseus is truly destined to return, let him come late, alone, having lost his companions, and find trouble at home (Od. 9.475--542).

The famous pun on ``Nobody'' is therefore not only a trick of identity. Anonymity temporarily insulates Odysseus from a particular form of retaliation. Once he gives Polyphemus his name, patronymic, and home, he makes the grievance addressable.

The irony is that Odysseus understands consequence perfectly when explaining the Cyclops's suffering. Polyphemus violated hospitality; his blinding is presented as repayment. Odysseus can see the antecedents of another man's suffering. Then he creates a new chain because he cannot bear for his victory to remain anonymous.

The desire for glory and the later experience of guilt have a common structure: both make it important that \emph{I am the one who did it}. Nolan's Odysseus eventually suffers under the same authorship he once demanded.

This is also where ``chance'' becomes especially unstable. Aristotle later defines \term{tyche} not as the absence of causation but as an incidental cause within purposive action (Physics II.5, 197a5--8). An outcome can be unintended relative to an agent's purpose and still have a perfectly real causal history. Nolan's own dialogue accidentally demonstrates the same point when Odysseus calls the dropped wind ``our chance.'' Chance can be an opening within a causal field, not its opposite.

\section{The survivor tells the causal history}

There is another problem in Books 9--12: most of the causal history reaches us through Odysseus himself. He is participant, commander, survivor, and retrospective narrator. His dead companions cannot provide competing accounts to the Phaeacians.

This does not mean he lies. It means causal claims have provenance. Loney's reading is valuable because it keeps track of focalization and of the poem's capacity to make Odysseus's triumphs ethically multivalent \citep{Loney2015}. Eurylochus's accusation in Book 10 matters precisely because it survives inside Odysseus's own account: even the commander's narration contains a rival assignment of blame.

Retrospection also changes the shape of decision. Before an event, several futures are practically available or at least imaginable. Afterward, one path has become actual. The survivor can connect the realized outcome backward to warnings and decisions that now appear decisive. This is not necessarily false. It is simply a different epistemic position from the one occupied by the actor before the result was known.

Odysseus's guilt repeatedly compresses that difference. He remembers the action through its terminal outcome. He remembers that he chose; he can forget how the wind, hunger, crew resistance, divine intervention, and incomplete information altered what the choice was at the time.

A stronger account of consequence therefore needs more than a causal arrow. It needs the perspective from which the arrow is being drawn.

\section{At Ithaca, Odysseus starts designing consequences}

After Book 13 the poem changes mode. Odysseus is no longer chiefly trying to survive consequences that exceed him. With Athena, he begins constructing the conditions under which consequences will happen to others.

The revenge plot is built through information asymmetry. Athena disguises Odysseus. Identity is revealed selectively. Telemachus removes weapons. Loyal servants are tested and then admitted to the plan. Doors are controlled. The bow passes into Odysseus's hands at the decisive moment. The suitors remain inside a room whose actual state they do not understand.

This does not manufacture their earlier wrongdoing. But it means their final destruction is not simply an event that ``comes back'' to them. It is staged.
